hi there steve kaufman here again um  language learning often gets involved  with politics it gets involved with  sports  um now we have the situation montreal  where the montreal canadiens or le  canadian  have a unilingual english-speaking coach  at least an interim coach because the  previous coach jacques martin who is  bilingual  was let go  and this has caused a great deal of  controversy in the french-speaking media  in in montreal  and uh they feel that the coach for the  montreal canadiens should be able to  speak in french where do i stand on this  you know and there's a lot of uh  commentary at least in the english  canadian media that it really shouldn't  matter that if he's a good coach then  all the fans will be happy as long as  the team wins and so forth  and we even had a discussion about it at  link in our forum and one of our members  ed  pointed out that um say if uh  a team in stockholm had a coach who  couldn't speak speak swedish nobody  would get upset  all of that is true however i very much  agree with the french speaking media  because professional sports is  entertainment  and every market is different  in sweden they are they don't expect  foreigners to speak swedish  there's no history of swedish swedish  having been sort of treated as a second  class language they haven't had to fight  for swedish language rights in sweden  so the whole political  environment and the situation in the  market and the expectations  of the consumers is quite different but  in montreal  it's a very sensitive subject  and  quite honestly in vancouver if we had a  unilingual french coach that would not  go down very well  i mean i follow the vancouver canucks  and every day or not every day but quite  often i go to their website and i watch  these little video interviews  typically the coach and the players have  nothing of interest to say  but we have a tendency to hang on to  their every word as if there was  substance there which there never is  it's always the same line they write the  script at the beginning of the year and  they all repeat it  but we want to hear these people say  something in a language that we can  understand so the swedish players we  have on our team when we had russians on  our team  checks whatever they all finns they can  all speak germans we've had they can all  speak english  uh and that's the language of the market  it's the same in golf i i some while ago  when there was this controversy over  whether the korean  uh the very successful korean women  golfers should be required to improve  their english and this was a request put  forward by  the lpga the ladies professional golfers  association and i said yes they should  they should have to they should this is  business  um of course i was i'm still they get  criticism on my youtube channel that i'm  a racist i'm a bigot and stuff like that  and to me it's it's just normal that's  it's business  when when they interview kj choi i don't  know if any of you follow golf but kj  choi who's a great golfer i love  watching him  he's got a very nice pleasant  decent manner and his english is  atrocious  but i love listening to him because it's  kj's voice it's him speaking  uh or trying to speak english and that's  far preferable to having  kj speak to an interpreter somebody  else's voice  and i remember some while ago there was  a fellow from argentina who won a major  golf uh tournament in the us and he said  well my golf speaks for itself and if i  play golf well that's all that matters  and now you shouldn't have to learn  english and uh he took all his  interviews in spanish well to my mind  he's a fool  because he's not helping out the whole  business model whereas other golfers  from spain from france from wherever  they come from japan  now kj for example in korea they will  try their best to say something in  english  now the coach getting back to the  montreal canadiens is the main  sort of contact point for the media  so yeah i would say that in the case of  the montreal canadiens with all the  tradition of their hockey teams which  used to be predominantly quebecois  french canadians and as now of course in  the in the new globalized nhl they could  be you know europeans or americans or  english canadians or anything else but  there is this tradition of lake nadia  you know  being  the team of the french canadians  so for that reason absolutely they  should have a coach who speaks french he  needn't be a franco  quebecois he can be anyone but who can  at least communicate in french and i  think the same would be true i'd be  curious to know if the coach of  the barcelona soccer teams  uh  speaks catalan  if he does if whoever he is if he  doesn't he should learn it  uh in order to communicate with the  people who pay his salaries so that's my  feeling on that and the the name of the  the interim coach who now looks like a  lame duck  cunningworth was it roger cunningworth  again we'd love to have him on link  uh they've got lots of time sitting in  airplanes and traveling around it  wouldn't take him very long  to uh to get to a level where he could  uh he could answer some questions  and uh and the emphasis should be  initially on understanding the language  uh  just so that there's so some sense that  he communicating uh with his marketplace  so that's my take on that yes the  montreal canadiens deserve a coach who  speaks french uh  professional sports is entertainment uh  it makes sense that i mean it's a  continuum and i see what the canucks do  here in vancouver they're forever  involved with the community they are  either out at some charity event or  they're at some fundraiser or there's a  dinner with supporters and they all  speak english because vancouver's an  english-speaking city and it seems to me  that the fans and supporters in montreal  have the right to the same  kind of entertainment and their  interaction with their stars in french  so there you have it thank you for  listening
 

